body:

One of my great school regrets is not taking statistics more seriously. But in my defence,
my statistics class was miserable, for a very stupid reason though: the lecturer had this
monotone voice that put me right to sleep. Sometimes I could not make out what he was saying clearly because
of his thick accent. So I missed the entire introductory lecture and did the bare minimum to pass,
cramming and forgetting everything I had learnt along the way.

Enter Einstein... thank you for making me feel better:

> "Education is what remains after one has forgotten what one has learned in school."

> Albert Einstein

Today I find myself using statistical methods routinely, so I thought I should attempt a
series of articles to help deepen my understanding of statistics.
In an age of Large Language Models, I also struggled with the why? Why would I want to do this to myself... 
I came to conclude that stats can just be plain fun... So...

## The spoonful and the pot

The one thing I cannot forget from my undergrad stats class is the story about soup and pots... 
My lecturer once said: think about it this way. You are making soup. It has been on the stove for an hour
and you want to know whether it needs more salt. You do not drink the whole pot to find out. 
You stir it, lift one spoonful, taste, and then decide what to do on the strength of one teaspoon...

That is statistics. One taste, and a conclusion about everything you did not taste. 

Now leave the kitchen for a government census. A country wants to know how many of its people
are employed. Nobody walks door to door counting every single one, that is what an actual
census is for, and even a census only happens once every ten years or so. In between, statisticians
run a survey instead: they sample a few thousand households, ask everyone the same questions, and
treat the answers as standing in for the whole country. A few thousand households standing in for
tens of millions of people. Same spoonful, bigger pot.

## Greater statistics

At its core, statistics is the science of learning from information. It gives you a set of
tools for describing, organising, and interpreting whatever you have in front of you.

<figure>
  <img src="./Leia.webp" alt="Princess Leia Organa">
  <figcaption>Princess Leia Organa holding the Death Star plans (Image via <a href="https://starwars.fandom.com/wiki/Death_Star_plans">Star Wars Fandom Wiki</a>)</figcaption>
</figure>

> "I only hope that when the data is analyzed, a weakness can be found."

> Leia Organa, *Star Wars: Episode IV - A New Hope*, 1977

Star Wars offers us a prime example of how using data effectively can result in major strategic wins!

I like the broader definition too, borrowed from David J. Hand's ***Statistics: A Very Short
Introduction*** (Hand, 2008), where he quotes the statistician John Chambers: *"Greater statistics
can be defined simply, if loosely, as everything related to learning from data, from the first
planning or collection to the last presentation or report."* Hand (2008) goes further, expanding on that core definition:
modern statistics is not really about calculation, it is about investigation. Statistics forms the foundation of any
problem-solving exercise, whether you know it or not...

From this, one can say, statistical ideas are everywhere, they sit underneath almost every part of life, sometimes obviously, sometimes not so obviously.
Either way, given how much of everything runs on statistical ideas, it is worth actually understanding them.
That is what I hope to achieve with this series, over time...

The hard part for me has been presenting stats in a way that feels intuitive rather than mechanical...
I remember once trying to explain to a colleague why taking the average of averages is incorrect...
She got more confused then mad, and eventually agreed with me just to end the conversation. I probably explained it badly,
because I still picture her out there doing it wrong somewhere...

### A word that used to mean paperwork

Long before anyone coined it statistics, counting was already useful. It told you the
seasons, weather patterns, how often the sun rose and set. It told you how much food you needed to
survive winter. It told you who owned what...

I read that statistics began as bookkeeping for governments. The word shares a root with "state" (Hand, 2008),
and for most of its history it meant exactly that: the tables of facts a state kept about itself.
Headcounts, births, deaths, grain, soldiers, tax... Nobody was trying to figure out anything,
they were just counting and filing.

The turn came in 1662, when John Graunt, a London haberdasher, generally considered to be the
founder of the science of demography (Encyclopaedia Britannica, n.d.), sat down with decades
of parish records of christenings and burials, the Bills of Mortality, and did something new with
them (Graunt, 1662).

<figure>
  <img src="./natural and polital observations.png" alt="Book Cover">
  <figcaption>Natural and Political Observations, 1662 Edition Cover
  (Image via <a href="https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_natural-and-political-ob_graunt-john-f-r-s_1662/page/(34)/mode/2up">Internet Archive</a>)</figcaption>
</figure>

He did not just tabulate the deaths. He used them to estimate things nobody had written down.
How many people lived in London, how many would survive to each age, how a plague year differed from an ordinary one (Graunt, 1662).
He reasoned from the numbers he had... to the numbers he wanted. That move, from the recorded to the unrecorded,
is the moment counting became statistics.

## The two jobs

Modern statistics still does both of Graunt's tasks, and it helps to keep them apart.
I have come to see statistics less as a set of formulas and more as a discipline for:
- collecting the data
- analysing the data
- presenting the data... in that order...

Get any one of them wrong and the rest does not matter.

Done properly, it is a **"bullshit detector"**. It lets you weigh a claim against the evidence behind it
and tell a reasonable conclusion from a dubious one. That matters more than ever now, given how much data gets
thrown around by people with an agenda...

Graunt provides us with the essense of statistics... the two jobs... descriptive and inferential statistics... (Salkind and Frey, 2026)

### Job #1: The tabulating job

**Descriptive statistics** is the tabulating. You have a set of numbers in front of you, a sample, and you summarise them:
the average, the spread, the shape. Nothing leaves the data on hand. If I tell you the twenty
surveyed students had an average mark of 68%, that is a fact about those twenty students and nothing else.

### Job #2: The reaching job

**Inferential statistics** is often, though not always, the next step after you have collected and summarised your data,
using a smaller group (say, twenty surveyed students) to say something about a larger one (every undergraduate at the
university).

Inferential statistics is Graunt's move. You use the numbers you have to make a claim about numbers you do not have:
the other 9,980 students, the population the sample was drawn from. The average of the sample (written x-bar)
becomes an estimate of the average of the whole cohort (written with the Greek letter mu).
<span class="margin-note"> "The convention is worth learning now, something I missed completely during my undergrad: Greek letters are the true values you cannot see, Roman letters are the ones you actually calculated.
The estimate is useful precisely because it reaches past the data." </span>

It is also where all the trouble lives, because the reach can be wrong.

| | Descriptive | Inferential |
|---|---|---|
| **Question** | What do these numbers say? | What do they let me guess about the numbers I don not have? |
| **Scope** | Only the data you collected | The whole population that data came from |
| **Example** | "These 20 students averaged 68%" | "The cohort of 10,000 averages about 68%, give or take" |
| **Risk** | Low, it is just arithmetic | Higher, it is a guess and can be wrong |

## The pot that you have tasted and the pot that is still cooking

W. Edwards Deming,
the statistician who spent the 1950s teaching Japanese manufacturers how to think about quality,
split statistical questions into two kinds (Deming, 1975).

An **enumerative question** which is about a fixed pile of things that already exists. How many students in this
cohort of ten thousand achieved a distinction, a mark above 75%? There is a real, exact answer sitting in the
registrar's records right now. Your sample is just a cheaper way of estimating it than checking all ten thousand transcripts.

An **analytic question** which is about a process that is still running. Will next year's incoming class also average above 75%?
There is no cohort to sample yet, because next year's students haven not enrolled. Your twenty surveyed students only tell
you about this year. They tell you about next year only if the admissions and teaching process stays stable. That
stability is an assumption, not something your sample can prove.

Almost every decision that matters is analytic. You are rarely trying to describe a cohort. You are trying to predict what
next year's cohort will look like, so you can act now. The arithmetic of the two is identical. The **confidence** you are entitled to is not.

## Which way the arrow points

One last piece of orientation, because it explains why this series will eventually have a companion on probability.

**Probability reasons forwards:** given a known model of the world, what data should I expect? If this coin is fair,
roughly half the flips come up heads. The model is assumed, the data is the unknown.

**Statistics reasons backwards:** given the data I actually observed, what model of the world is plausible?
I flipped it a hundred times and got seventy heads... is it still reasonable to call this coin fair? The data is fixed,
the model is the unknown.

Same coin, opposite arrows. Probability hands you the model and asks about the data.
Statistics hands you the data and asks about the model. The backwards direction is harder, and less certain,
and the one I spend my working life in...

## The one assumption holding it all up

Every inference rests on one quiet condition: that the sample looks like the population,
because it was drawn without favour. Stir the pot and the spoonful represents the soup.
Leave it unstirred and you taste whatever settled at the top. Pull your twenty students from the front row of one
lecture hall and you have measured that lecture hall, not the whole cohort. When statistics goes wrong, this is usually where it started.


## Closing the lid

Statistics is the art of the spoonful. You are always deciding about the pot you did not taste.


## References

1. Deming, W.E. (1975) On probability as a basis for action. *The American Statistician*, 29(4), pp.146-152. The enumerative vs. analytic split, straight from the source.

2. Graunt, J. (1662) *Natural and Political Observations Made upon the Bills of Mortality*. London: Thomas Roycroft. Available at: https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_natural-and-political-ob_graunt-john-f-r-s_1662 (Accessed: 23 September 2026).

3. Encyclopaedia Britannica (n.d.) *John Graunt*. Available at: https://www.britannica.com/biography/John-Graunt (Accessed: 23 September 2026).

4. Hand, D.J. (2008) *Statistics: A Very Short Introduction*. Oxford: Oxford University Press.

5. Salkind, N.J. and Frey, B.B. (2026) *Statistics for People Who (Think They) Hate Statistics*. 8th edn. Thousand Oaks, CA: SAGE Publications.
